A single pass is enough here. Start with the first number as the current maximum, then update it whenever a larger value appears.

This does not change `nums`, and it also works when all values are negative.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int largestElement(vector<int>& nums) {
        int largest = nums[0];

        for (int value : nums) {
            if (value > largest) {
                largest = value;
            }
        }

        return largest;
    }
};
```

For `[4, 9, 2, 7]`, the current answer changes from `4` to `9`, then stays `9` for the remaining values.

Time complexity: `O(n)`
Space complexity: `O(1)`

A max priority queue keeps the greatest number at the front. Every input value is added, and the value at the top is returned at the end.

A heap is not needed for such a simple task, so this uses more memory and takes more time than a scan. It is included as a direct priority-queue version.

```java
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

class Solution {
    public int largestElement(int[] nums) {
        PriorityQueue<Integer> maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> Integer.compare(b, a));

        for (int value : nums) {
            maxHeap.offer(value);
        }

        return maxHeap.peek();
    }
}
```

For `[3, 8, 5]`, the heap keeps `8` at the front, so `peek()` gives the answer. Duplicate values do not cause any issue.

