The array is split into two parts until each part has one value. Each call returns the larger result from its left and right parts.

This is a recursive version, so it is less practical than a loop, but it shows how the answer can be built from smaller ranges. The input array remains unchanged.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int largestElement(vector<int>& nums) {
        return findLargest(nums, 0, static_cast<int>(nums.size()) - 1);
    }

private:
    int findLargest(vector<int>& nums, int left, int right) {
        if (left == right) {
            return nums[left];
        }

        int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;
        int leftLargest = findLargest(nums, left, middle);
        int rightLargest = findLargest(nums, middle + 1, right);

        return max(leftLargest, rightLargest);
    }
};
```

Dry run for `[6, 2, 9, 4]`: the left half gives `6`, the right half gives `9`, and the final comparison returns `9`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`
Space complexity: `O(log n)` for the recursion stack

